wasm diag: idle-warp, module-economy and range-flush counters; compaction knobs

Measurement infrastructure for the 2026-09-12 profile session, plus two
env knobs so the benchmark can reach settings that were compile-time
constants (the playbook's rule: if the page has a knob, the benchmark
must be able to set it).

Counters (all cold - at most ~90k increments per boot, no hot path):
  PHYS_CALL/SCAN/DROP   tlb_flush_phys_ranges calls / entries walked /
                        entries invalidated (used to size 0043)
  MOD_BYTES/MOD_COUNT   bytes handed to WebAssembly.Module, modules made
  TB_BYTES              sum of emitted TB body sizes
  CLOSE/COMPACT/ENSURE  those bytes split by assemble source
  WARP_NS + 7 buckets   ns the idle warp jumped, bucketed by size

Knobs: W64_COMPACT_BATCHES / W64_COMPACT_MEMBERS (were #defines).

What they measured on a S75v40lg1 boot (dist-jit, rt=off):
  - 197 MB of wasm compiled per boot over 35k modules, of which 94 MB is
    unique TB bodies: 563 B of wasm per 4.85-insn TB, compiled 2.1x.
  - the second compile is compaction: 96 MB in 198 modules, i.e. ~49 % of
    all bytes the browser compiles.  Turning compaction off halves the
    bytes and measured NEUTRAL (see the playbook's REJECTED table).
  - the boot's virtual timeline is ~42 s, 31.5 s of it idle warp, almost
    all of it produced after t0.5G (the display-DMA stretch warps ~18 s of
    virtual time in ~4 s of wall).  Under the shipping rt=banked cap that
    warp is paid as real sleep: t1.3G 38.9 s vs 29.3 s at rt=off (+33 %).

